  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset titled \"Choanoeca flexa transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteome\" has garnered attention within the academic community, evidenced by a citation in a notable journal article. It contributes to the understanding of biological concepts, specifically within the categories of transcriptomics and proteomics, aligning with sustainable development goals related to addressing hunger.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This dataset, published in 2019, presents a comprehensive transcriptome and predicted proteome for the organism Choanoeca flexa. Its open access nature facilitates ongoing research in computational biology, genetics, and related fields. Despite limited visibility\u2014with just 16 views and 5 downloads\u2014the dataset has made an early mark, as indicated by a single citation in 2025 from the journal eLife.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Adopting a scholarly approach, this dataset demonstrates a connection to global sustainability challenges, notably linked to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goal of Zero Hunger. The relevance of the research firmly positions it within the discourse on how biological tools and understanding can help address critical food security issues.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">With a contribution from Thibaut Brunet as the corresponding author, the dataset signifies collaborative efforts in academic research. Although there is no broad social media mention or extensive community discourse noted, the scholarly citation indicates a foundational impact that may pave the way for further studies examining protein-related functionalities in metabolic pathways crucial for understanding hunger-related biologies.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">As this dataset continues to be referenced, it may serve as a critical resource for researchers exploring evolutionary biology and protein-coupled receptors, thereby enriching the existing literature and contributing to more extensive academic inquiries in the future.</p>",
